   "body": "<html><title>How Laws Are Made in Denmark: The Legislative Process | Danify | Danify</title><body><p>Back to blog Danish citizenship test Folketing Danish parliament how laws are made in Denmark Danish democracy legislative process Denmark Danish citizenship preparation</p>\n<p>How Laws Are Made in Denmark: The Legislative Process Explained AI AI-generated</p>\n<p>Curious about how Denmark creates its laws? Learn about the Danish legislative process, the role of the Folketing, and what you need to know for the citizenship test.</p>\n<p>Published on May 1, 2026 5 min read</p>\n<p>Understanding Danish Democracy Through Its Laws</p>\n<p>Denmark is widely regarded as one of the world's most well-functioning democracies, and at the heart of that reputation lies a transparent, structured process for making laws. Whether you are preparing for the Danish citizenship test or simply want to understand how your new home country works, learning how legislation is created is essential. The Danish legislative process reflects core values of democracy, accountability, and civic participation — values that are deeply embedded in Danish society and culture.</p>\n<p>In this guide, we will walk you through every stage of how a law is made in Denmark, from the initial proposal to the royal signature that makes it official.</p>\n<p>The Folketing: Denmark's Parliament</p>\n<p>All legislation in Denmark originates in or passes through Folketinget — the Danish Parliament. Located at Christiansborg Palace in Copenhagen, the Folketing is the supreme legislative authority in the country.</p>\n<p>The Folketing consists of 179 members (called Members of Parliament or folket</p>\n</body></html>"
  },
  "http://127.0.0.1:8888/search?q=Danish+parliament+legislative+process+three+readings+explained&format=json&language=all": {
   "body": "{\"results\": [{\"title\": \"How Laws Are Made in Denmark: The Legislative Process | Danify ...\", \"url\": \"https://danify.dk/en/blog/how-laws-are-made-in-denmark-the-legislative-process-explain-momkd6pp/\", \"content\": \"May 1, 2026 · Once a bill is introduced, it must pass through three formal readings (behandlinger) in the Folketing before it can become law. This structured process ensures that legislation is carefully examined and debated.\"}, {\"title\": \"The Parliament / The Danish Parliament\", \"url\": \"https://www.thedanishparliament.dk/en/democracy/the-parliament\", \"content\": \"The legislative process The Parliament and the Government hold the legislative power. The Government and the Members of Parliament can propose new laws, but only Parliament can pass the proposed laws (Bills). Each year, the Danish Parliament reads around 200 Bills. Laws regulate: how we live together in Denmark what we do together how the public (e.g., public schools and hospitals) must be ...\"}, {\"title\": \"PDF The Danish Parliament and EU affairs - europarl.europa.eu\", \"url\": \"https://www.europarl.europa.eu/RegData/etudes/BRIE/2025/772873/EPRS_BRI(2025)772873_EN.pdf\", \"content\": \"at national level; and, as part of the Danish Realm, Greenland · and the Faroe Islands each elect two members. Both the government and Members of Parliament can introduce · new legislation, but only the Parliament can pass the proposed · laws. The legislative process is designed to provide time for · debate and reflection. A bill cannot be finally adopted before · three readings in the Folketing, and 40 days must have passed ·\"}, {\"title\": \"The Danish Folketing: How Denmark's Parliament Works | Danify | Danify\", \"url\": \"https://danify.dk/en/blog/the-danish-folketing-how-denmarks-parliament-works-msob9kzo/\", \"content\": \"Aug 11, 2026 · Understanding the legislative process is vital for citizenship exam preparation. Here's how a bill becomes law in Denmark: This three-reading system ensures thorough deliberation and represents the Danish commitment to careful, democratic lawmaking.\"}, {\"title\": \"The Danish Parliament (Folketinget) Explained for Expats 2026\", \"url\": \"https://denmarkexpat.com/the-danish-parliament-explained/\", \"content\": \"Key Functions and Roles Legislative Process: How Laws are Proposed and Passed Laws can be proposed by the government or by individual MPs. The standard stages are: First reading: General debate of the bill's purpose. Committee stage: Detailed examination by the relevant committee; experts and stakeholders may be consulted.\"}, {\"title\": \"Danish language - Wikipedia\", \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Danish_language\", \"content\": \"Danish itself can be divided into three main dialect areas: Jutlandic (West Danish), Insular Danish (including the standard variety), …Read more\"}, {\"title\": \"Democracy - The Danish Parliament\", \"url\": \"https://www.thedanishparliament.dk/en/Democracy\", \"content\": \"Democracy The Parliament The Parliament's main tasks are to process proposed legislation and pass the nation's laws, to control the government's use of power and to process and pass the state's yearly budget.\"}, {\"title\": \"PDF FOLKETINGET - THE DANISH PARLIAMENT - ft.dk\", \"url\": \"https://www.ft.dk/-/media/sites/ft/pdf/publikationer/folketinget-the-danish-parliament.pdf\", \"content\": \"In order to ensure a stable democracy and to prevent misuse of power, the supreme power in Denmark is, like in most other Western democra-cies, divided into three independent organs which control one another i.e. the legislative, the executive and the judicial powers.\"}]}"
  },
  "https://denmarkexpat.com/the-danish-parliament-explained/": {
   "body": "<html><title>The Danish Parliament (Folketinget) Explained for Expats 2026</title><body><p>How the Danish Parliament (Folketinget) Works – What Expats Need to Know 2026</p>\n<p>This content contain affiliate or advertising links. If you click and make a purchase, we may receive a commission. Your price is not affected.</p>\n<p>Want to know how decisions in the Folketing will affect your job, taxes, benefits or stay in Denmark? This guide cuts through the formality and shows you what matters to you as an expat: who makes laws, how they get passed, and the quickest ways to follow or influence them.</p>\n<p>Seats: 179 members (175 Denmark, 2 Faroe Islands, 2 Greenland).</p>\n<p>Elections: maximum four-year terms; the prime minister can call elections earlier.</p>\n<p>Electoral threshold: 2% national vote for a party to qualify for leveling seats.</p>\n<p>Only Danish citizens may vote in Folketing elections; EU and other residents have municipal/EU voting rights in different contexts.</p>\n<p>The Danish Parliament Explained: Historical Context</p>\n<p>Evolution and Development of the Danish Parliament</p>\n<p>The Folketing grew from medieval advisory councils such as the Danehof, but the modern system starts with the 1849 Constitution (Grundloven). That constitution replaced absolute monarchy with a constitutional monarchy and introduced parliamentary institutions. Parliamentary practice continued to evolve across the 19th and 20th centuries, moving power from the crown to elected representatives and building the institutional framework that underpins Danish democracy today.</p>\n<p>Key Events that Have Shaped its Current Form</p>\n<p>Several changes resh</p>\n</body></html>"
